## 4. Approach 2: Using an Extra Array

We can create a new array and calculate the final position of every element.

### Main Idea

When rotating right by `k` positions, an element originally at index `i` moves to:

```java
newIndex = (i + k) % n;
```

For example:

```text
nums = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7]
k = 3
n = 7
```

The element at index zero moves to:

```text
(0 + 3) % 7 = 3
```

The element at index four moves to:

```text
(4 + 3) % 7 = 0
```

### Java Code

```java
import java.util.Arrays;

public class RotateArrayExtraSpace {

    public static void rotate(int[] nums, int k) {

        int n = nums.length;

        k = k % n;

        int[] result = new int[n];

        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            int newIndex = (i + k) % n;

            result[newIndex] = nums[i];
        }

        // Copy result back into original array
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {

            nums[i] = result[i];
        }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {

        int[] nums = {1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7};

        rotate(nums, 3);

        System.out.println(Arrays.toString(nums));
    }
}
```

**Output:**

```text
[5, 6, 7, 1, 2, 3, 4]
```

### Complexity Analysis

- Time Complexity: O(n).
- Extra Space Complexity: O(n), because we create another array.

This is efficient, but it does not satisfy the O(1) extra-space requirement.

