## 13. Interview Questions

### Q1. What is array rotation?

Array rotation means moving elements from one end of an array to the other while maintaining their relative order.

### Q2. Why do we use `k % n`?

Because rotating an array `n` times returns it to its original position.

Therefore, rotations greater than `n` can be reduced using modulo.

### Q3. What is the reversal algorithm?

It is an in-place technique that uses multiple reversals to rearrange array elements.

### Q4. Why do we reverse the entire array first?

This places the last `k` elements at the beginning, but their internal order is reversed. The next two reversals restore the correct order.

### Q5. What is the time complexity?

O(n), because each element participates in a constant number of swaps.

### Q6. What is the space complexity?

O(1), because we modify the original array without creating another array.

### Q7. Can we solve this problem using an extra array?

Yes. We can calculate the new index of each element using:

```java
newIndex = (i + k) % n;
```

But that requires O(n) extra space.

### Q8. What is the difference between left and right rotation?

- Right rotation moves the last elements to the beginning.
- Left rotation moves the first elements to the end.

