## 15. Final Summary

We learned three approaches:

1. Brute Force – rotate one position at a time.
2. Extra Array – calculate the new index of every element.
3. Reversal Algorithm – reverse the entire array and then reverse its two sections.

### Recommended Interview Solution

```java
public void rotate(int[] nums, int k) {

    int n = nums.length;

    if (n == 0) {
        return;
    }

    k = k % n;

    reverse(nums, 0, n - 1);

    reverse(nums, 0, k - 1);

    reverse(nums, k, n - 1);
}

private void reverse(int[] nums, int left, int right) {

    while (left < right) {

        int temp = nums[left];

        nums[left] = nums[right];

        nums[right] = temp;

        left++;
        right--;
    }
}
```

**Remember these important concepts:**

- Use modulo to handle rotations greater than the array length.
- Use two pointers to reverse an array.
- Use the reversal algorithm for in-place rotation.
- Always check for an empty array before using modulo.

**Important interview takeaway:** The reversal algorithm is a useful pattern for array rotation and can also be applied to string and sequence manipulation problems.
